---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import MistakeExhibits from '../../components/MistakeExhibits.astro'
import mistakes from '../../data/xla-mistakes.json'
---

<Base
  title="The mistake museum · the XLA wing — Rudrite Kernels"
  description="Real failures at the SPMD seam, reproduced and captured on eight host devices: uneven shardings, unbound mesh axes, collectives without a mesh."
>
  <div class="ws-ground">
    <section class="wband">
      <div class="wrap wgrid">
        <span class="wtag">the museum<br />xla wing</span>
        <div>
          <p class="mono-label serves">serves the xla path · reproduced on 8 host-platform devices (jax 0.4.38, CPU, 2026-07-27), fixes run and proven · <a class="wing-link" href="/mistakes">all wings</a></p>
          <h1 class="wh1">The partitioner says no, and it means something.</h1>
          <p class="wlede">
            Three exhibits from the SPMD seam, where your sharding meets the compiler's rules:
            a spec the shape cannot satisfy, an axis the mesh does not have, and a collective
            called with no mesh in sight. All three reproduce on a laptop with eight fake host
            devices. The wing is young on purpose: compile-side failures worth exhibiting (memory
            exhaustion, layout conflicts) only speak truthfully from a real chip. LAB·X2 on the
            fusion chapter captures exactly those on a Colab TPU, and its paste-backs become
            this wing's next exhibits.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <MistakeExhibits entries={mistakes} />
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .serves { margin-bottom: 1rem; letter-spacing: 0.04em; }
  .wing-link { color: var(--copper); text-decoration: none; }
  .wing-link:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
</style>
